  ],
  "text": "A banknote costs a few rupees to print. It is worth what is printed on it because everyone acts like it is. Money used to be the thing itself. Salt, cattle, metal with real value inside it. Then paper that promised the metal. Hand the paper in and you get the gold. Then the promise quietly went away, and the paper stayed exactly where it was. So what is holding it up now? Two things. Trust in whoever issued it, and a law saying debts can be settled with it. That is it. There is nothing else under there at all. Which is not a scandal. A shared agreement is a real thing for as long as it is shared. Hyperinflation is what happens when it stops being shared. The paper does not change at all. The note in your pocket is a collective agreement that you carry around. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
